WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.860
Welcome back to Models and Agents, Episode 124 for July 27, 2026.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.240
Your daily AI briefing, new week in AI, and if last week was anything to go by,

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.880
buckle up. Let's get into it. Autonomous support agents just prove they can handle real volume.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.360
G2A's Dave closed 14,400 tickets without human help.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:31.140
G2A deployed an autonomous AI agent named Dave that resolved 14,400 seller support tickets

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.900
over 63 days. Meta confirmed it will release a new open source model.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:43.960
Quen3.627b showed stronger speculative decoding gains on heavier quantization levels.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.240
Builders should watch how these agent and inference improvements translate to production

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:56.820
workloads this week. G2A.com deployed its autonomous AI agent Dave to handle seller support

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:04.500
tickets. The agent resolved 14,400 tickets across 63 days, with no reported human intervention

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.600
in the loop. This demonstrates concrete throughput for a narrow but high-volume customer support

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.420
workflow. The result gives teams a data point on what fully autonomous ticket resolution looks

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:22.200
like in practice today. Companies running similar high-ticket volume operations should test

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.960
comparable agent setups against their own support logs this week. Watch for follow-up metrics

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:36.400
on resolution quality and escalation rates. Quen3.627b showed the largest speculative decoding speed-ups

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:44.540
on Q8 weights, with gains decreasing through Q6 and Q4. 10 of 10 tested speculative configurations

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:51.400
ranked Q8 highest by multiplier. Acceptance rates stayed consistent across matched quant depths

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.600
while base steps speed dropped with higher weight bite counts. D-flash delivered the highest absolute

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:06.520
tokens per second overall. MTP ranked second across most setups. The NVFP4 SG-Lang pair proved

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:13.300
fastest overall among quant and engine combinations. Weaver appeared as an outlier because it is fork

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:21.940
only with per target heads, limiting it to specific model and quant pairings. Lama.cpp's MTP path

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:30.580
on UDQ4 ran pathologically slow, producing lower absolute tokens per second than the Q6 MTP3

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:37.900
variant at identical acceptance rates. Builders running local inference on 27b class models should

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:43.620
benchmark D-flash or MTP on their target quant this week to capture the reported gains.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:50.920
The tests used short outputs, greedy decoding and batch size one, so the multipliers represent

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:56.800
an upper bound rather than a general concurrency result. The announcement came via a screenshot

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.620
of a post by Alexander Wang. No architecture details, parameter count or release timeline were

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:09.460
provided in the post. Community discussion centers on how the new model will compare to existing

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:15.280
open weight families. Developers tracking open releases should monitor meta's official channels

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:22.020
for the first weight drop. Siemens introduced a characterization AI agent that automates design

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:28.480
iteration loops. The agent targets electronics design workflows where repeated characterization steps

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:35.380
are common. No specific performance numbers or integration details were released. Hardware and

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:42.300
EDA teams should watch for the first public demos or API access. Islands launched as a shared

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:49.180
economic network where autonomous AI agents and humans can work, trade and co-evolve. The platform

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:54.960
positions itself as infrastructure for agent to agent and human to agent economic interactions.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.860
No technical architecture, APIs or early access details were disclosed. Teams exploring multi-agent

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:08.880
marketplaces should track the project's first public documentation. Simon Willison posted that

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.080
image segmentation capabilities similar to those demonstrated in Gemini 2.5 would be useful.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.060
The tweet links to his earlier write-up on Gemini image segmentation. No new implementation was

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:27.560
shared. Developers working on multi-modal tooling should review the linked post for context on

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:33.740
current segmentation behavior. Sam Altman shared a detailed prompt that used full chat history

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:40.140
to generate three trip options. Build a coordination site, reach group consensus and draft reservation

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:47.240
emails. The system completed the end-to-end task from a mobile prompt. The example illustrates current

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:53.160
multi-step agent planning reach. Builders should test similar long horizon prompts against their

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:59.240
own coordination workflows. Okay, let's pop the hood on parameter efficient fine-tuning for

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:06.200
mixture of experts models. Everyone talks about uniform adapters as the obvious default. Yet Moe

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:11.560
squared Laura couples the pre-trained router directly to task specific Laura routing through a

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:17.680
mobile channel routing condition projection module. The router activations already computed by

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.900
the base model are reused to decide which Laura experts activate, avoiding a second full routing

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:30.420
pass. A single global Laura expert pool is shared across all layers, allowing emergent,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.680
layer-wise affinities while keeping total added parameters low. This design reduces the risk of

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.080
overriding base expert specialization compared with static adapter placement. The approach delivered

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.900
state-of-the-art downstream accuracy on multiple Moe backbones while preserving more general

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:57.280
capabilities than prior Moe PEFT methods. Teams fine-tuning Moe models should test Moe squared Laura

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.760
first when router reuse is feasible. The main gotcha is ensuring the base router remains frozen

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:11.360
during adaptation, so routing priors are not disturbed. Test D-flash or MTP speculative decoding on

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.760
Quinn 3.627b at Q8 weights to measure the reported speed-ups on your hardware.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:24.680
Review Simon Willisons Gemini image segmentation write-up if you need multimodal region extraction

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:31.560
in current workflows. Run Sam Altman's group trip prompt style against your own multi-step

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:37.840
planning tasks to see where current agents succeed or break. Monitor Meta's channels for the

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.820
promised open-source model weights and compare early releases against Quinn and Lama families on

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:50.500
your target tasks. Simon's characterization agent expected to reach public demos or early access.

